@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex what Harmless is
Harmless is a coding harness written in Emacs Lisp.  It is not gptel.
It does not wrap Grok Build, Claude Code, or the Codex CLI.  It talks
to a model provider itself, keeps the transcript in Emacs, and runs
tools in the project directory.

A session is one conversation.  It is pinned to one connection and one
model.  Several sessions can run in one Emacs.  One can use Grok,
another a local model, and a third Claude, each in its own project.

The transcript buffer is a view.  The saved session on disk is the
record.  Closing the buffer does not delete the session.

Harmless requires GNU Emacs 31.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@findex harmless-setup
Put the @file{lisp} directory on @code{load-path} and load the package.

@example
(add-to-list 'load-path "~/programming/emacs/harmless/lisp")
(require 'harmless)
@end example

@vindex harmless-providers
@vindex harmless-default-model
@vindex harmless-default-reasoning-effort
@vindex harmless-default-provider-name
Choose the connections Harmless may use.  Most people need one.

@example
(setq harmless-providers (list (harmless-make-xai))
      harmless-default-model "grok-4.6"
      harmless-default-reasoning-effort "xhigh")
@end example

@findex harmless-make-xai
@findex harmless-make-openai
@findex harmless-make-anthropic
@findex harmless-make-openai-compat
The constructors are @code{harmless-make-xai},
@code{harmless-make-openai}, @code{harmless-make-anthropic}, and
@code{harmless-make-openai-compat}.  Each takes an optional connection
name.  The default names are @samp{xAI}, @samp{OpenAI}, and
@samp{Anthropic}.  A second account needs its own name, such as
@samp{xAI work}.

@vindex harmless-directory
Settings can live in your init file or in @file{config.el} under
@code{harmless-directory}.  That directory defaults to @file{harmless}
inside @code{user-emacs-directory}, so a typical path is
@file{~/.emacs.d/harmless/config.el}.  Harmless loads @file{config.el}
once, the first time a command needs it.

If @code{harmless-providers} is empty, @kbd{M-x harmless} asks you to
pick a provider and a connection name.

A local OpenAI-compatible server looks like this:

@example
(setq harmless-providers
      (list (harmless-make-openai-compat
             "local"
             :host "127.0.0.1:11434"
             :protocol "http"
             :endpoint "/v1/chat/completions"
             :key "none"
             :models '("llama3.2"))))
@end example

@node Starting
@chapter Starting

@findex harmless
@kindex M-x harmless
@kbd{M-x harmless} opens the session for the current project.  A
prefix argument opens the dashboard instead.

The current project is the project root Emacs would use for the
buffer, or @code{default-directory} when the buffer is not in a
project.  The directory must be absolute.  Harmless refuses to treat a
missing directory as the filesystem root.

On the first run, sign in when the provider asks.  @xref{Accounts}.

@node Sessions
@chapter Sessions

@findex harmless-current
@findex harmless-new
@findex harmless-chat
@findex harmless-switch
@findex harmless-dashboard
@cindex session
@cindex detached chat

@table @kbd
@item M-x harmless-current
Open or resume the newest session for this project.  If none exists,
start one with the default connection and model.

@item M-x harmless-new
Start a new session in the current project.  Harmless asks for the
connection, the model, and the reasoning effort when more than one
choice exists.

@item M-x harmless-chat
Start a chat with no project and no working directory.  This is a
detached chat.  It can use global memory and ask you a question.  It
cannot read project files, run a shell, or enter plan mode.

@item M-x harmless-switch
Jump to a live or saved session.  Completion shows the project, the
title, and the session id.

@item M-x harmless-dashboard
List every session, grouped by project.  @xref{Keys}, for the
dashboard keys.
@end table

The first user message becomes the title, cut at 60 characters, until
you set a title when you create the session.  A set title stays.

The header line of the transcript shows the title (or the project
name), the connection, the model, the status, and the word
@samp{plan} while plan mode is on.  @xref{Plan mode}.

Two sessions in one project get separate transcript buffers.  The
buffer name is @code{*harmless: @var{project}*} when the project has
one session, and @code{*harmless: @var{project} @var{id}*} when it has
more.  A detached chat always includes the id.

@node Transcript
@chapter Transcript

@findex harmless-session-mode
@kindex TAB @r{(transcript)}
@kindex S-TAB @r{(transcript)}
@kindex LEFT @r{(transcript)}
@kindex RIGHT @r{(transcript)}
The transcript is read-only.  Replies are Markdown.  A tool call is a
folded line.  A run of reads and searches folds into one
@samp{explored} line.

@table @kbd
@item TAB
Toggle the fold at point.

@item S-TAB
Toggle every fold.

@item LEFT
Close the fold at point.

@item RIGHT
Open the fold at point.

@item RET
Move to the prompt.

@item i
Move to the prompt.

@item q
Quit the window.

@item k
Compact the transcript.

@item w
Rewind to the turn at point.

@item E
Edit the prompt at point and rerun from there.

@item f
Fork through the turn at point.
@end table

@findex harmless-compact
@vindex harmless-compact-keep-turns
@vindex harmless-compact-threshold
@kindex k @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{k}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-compact}, summarizes the older turns and
replaces them with one summary.  The recent turns stay in the
transcript.  A turn starts at a user message and includes the
assistant reply and tool results that follow it.  The default keeps
the last two turns (@code{harmless-compact-keep-turns}).  A numeric
prefix argument sets how many turns to keep, and the command keeps at
least one.  An empty summary leaves the transcript as it was.  A turn
that is streaming or waiting on permission is left alone.  The same
command is @kbd{k} in the Harmless menu.

When the last prompt has used at least @code{harmless-compact-threshold}
percent of the model's context window, the next prompt compacts first.
The default is 80.  The same percent applies to every model.  A model
with a larger window compacts at the same fullness.  Nil turns
automatic compact off.  A model with no known window is left alone.
An automatic compact that fails still sends the prompt.

@findex harmless-rewind
@kindex w @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{w}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-rewind}, drops every turn after the one
at point and saves the shorter transcript.  That turn stays, including
its assistant reply and tool results.  A numeric prefix argument keeps
that many turns from the start of the transcript.  Outside the
transcript, the command asks which turn to keep.  It keeps at least
one turn and asks before dropping anything.  The dropped turns are not
saved.  The last prompt size is cleared, and the running token totals
stay.  A turn that is streaming or waiting on permission is left
alone.  The same command is @kbd{w} in the Harmless menu.

@findex harmless-edit-prompt
@kindex E @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{E}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-edit-prompt}, replaces the prompt at point
and sends the new text.  The reply to the old prompt, and every turn
after it, is dropped and is not saved.  A numeric prefix argument
chooses the turn, counting from the start of the transcript.  Outside
the transcript, the command asks which turn to edit.  It asks before
changing anything.  The last prompt size is cleared, and the running
token totals stay.  A turn that is streaming or waiting on permission
is left alone.  The same command is @kbd{E} in the Harmless menu.

@findex harmless-fork
@kindex f @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{f}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-fork}, copies the transcript through the
turn at point into a new session.  That turn stays in the copy,
including its assistant reply and tool results.  Later turns stay in
the original session.  The original session is left unchanged.
Nothing is sent.  A numeric prefix argument chooses the turn, counting
from the start of the transcript.  Outside the transcript, the command
asks which turn to copy through.  It asks before creating the new
session.  The copy starts with empty token totals.  A turn that is
streaming or waiting on permission is left alone.  The same command is
@kbd{f} in the Harmless menu.

Click the model name in the header line to change the connection, the
model, and the effort.  @xref{Models}.

@node Prompt
@chapter Prompt

@findex harmless-prompt-send
@findex harmless-steer
@findex harmless-prompt-mode
@kindex C-c C-c
@kindex C-c RET
The prompt is a small window under the transcript.  Type there and
send the turn.

@table @kbd
@item C-c C-c
Send the prompt.  @kbd{C-c @key{RET}} does the same.  While a turn is
streaming, this queues the prompt instead of aborting the turn.

@item C-c C-a
Abort the in-flight turn or shell.  @xref{Trouble}.

@item C-c C-k
Open the Harmless menu.  @xref{Keys}.
@end table

An empty prompt is refused: @samp{Prompt is empty}.  Nil, @code{""},
and whitespace are the same refusal.  While a permission question is
showing, send is refused until you answer it.  @xref{Permissions}.

@kbd{M-x harmless-steer} reads a follow-up from the minibuffer and
queues it without aborting the turn.  That includes while a permission
question is showing.  The same empty-prompt refusal applies.  A value
such as @code{/}, @code{c:/}, or @code{sub/} is text, and it is queued.

The follow-up is sent after the current tool batch, before the next
request to the model.  A reply that used no tools is followed by the
queued text, and the turn continues.  Several follow-ups keep the
order they were queued.  Abort drops a queue that has not been sent.

@node Permissions
@chapter Permissions

@findex harmless-set-permission-mode
@vindex harmless-permission-mode
@cindex permission
@kindex y @r{(permission)}
@kindex n @r{(permission)}
@kindex ! @r{(permission)}
Each tool belongs to a class: @code{read}, @code{edit},
@code{shell}, or @code{network}.  A read never asks.  An edit asks.
A shell asks.  A network call asks.

A @code{run_shell} command that Harmless can see is read-only is
treated as a read, so it does not ask.  A command Harmless does not
recognize still asks.  A redirect that writes, a command
substitution, and a subshell still ask.  @code{shell_kill} still
asks.  In plan mode a read-only shell command still runs, and a
shell command that can write is refused before the question.
@xref{Plan mode}.
A file watch does not ask.  A command that can write asks, and the
question shows that command.  @xref{Monitors}.
Scheduling a prompt asks, and the question shows that prompt.
Accepting edits does not ask.  @xref{Schedules}.

The permission mode of a session changes that.

@table @code
@item ask
The default.  Edits, shell commands, and network calls ask.

@item accept-edits
Edits proceed.  Shell commands and network calls still ask.

@item always-approve
Edits, shell commands, and network calls proceed.

@item auto
A reviewer decides an escalation.  A call that would ask is
checked first.  @code{allow} skips the question.
@code{escalate} asks.  An allow rule skips the reviewer.  A
deny, a dangerous command, and a shell write in plan mode still
refuse before the reviewer.  @code{ask}, @code{accept-edits},
and @code{always-approve} do not call the reviewer.  A read
still does not ask.
@end table

@kbd{M-x harmless-set-permission-mode} sets the mode for the current
session.  The menu key is @kbd{p}.  New sessions take the value of
@code{harmless-permission-mode}, which defaults to @code{ask}.

When a tool needs a decision, the prompt shows the class and a short
description of the call.  For an edit, that description is the path.
For a shell command, it is the command.  For a web search, it is the
query.  For a fetched page, it is the URL.  For an X search, it
is the query or the post id.  For an MCP call, it is the resource
uri or the server name.  For a click, it is the point.  For typed
text, it is the text.  For a key, it is the key.  For a browser,
it is the URL.  A screenshot shows the tool name.  A file
watch does not ask.  A command that can write shows the command.
A scheduled prompt shows the prompt.  Cancelling one shows the id.

@table @kbd
@item y
Allow this call.

@item n
Deny this call.  The model receives an error and can try something else.

@item !
Allow this class for the rest of the session.
@end table

Those three keys insert themselves when no question is pending, so you
can type them in an ordinary prompt.

A denied call does not change the project.  An allowed edit or shell
command runs at once.

@vindex harmless-permission-rules
@vindex harmless-permission-denies
@code{harmless-permission-rules} and
@code{harmless-permission-denies} are lists of rules copied onto a
session when it is created and when it is loaded.  A rule set only
on that session is not saved, and loading the session replaces it
with these lists.

@file{.harmless/permissions} in the project is an allow list that
survives the session.  One rule per line.  A blank line is
ignored.  A line that starts with @code{#} is a comment.  Those
rules are copied onto every session in that project, including
one that is loaded again.  A detached chat has no project file.
Nil, @code{""}, a relative path, and the filesystem root are not
a project.  A missing file adds no rules and creates nothing.  A
directory at that path is refused: @samp{permissions file is a
directory}.  A bad line is refused with the file path, a colon,
and the rule error.  The file is not copied into the session,
and saving the session does not write it.

A rule is @code{Bash(PATTERN)}, @code{Edit(PATTERN)}, or
@code{WebFetch(domain:HOST)}.  @code{Bash(npm run *)} matches a
shell command, and the same form matches a command monitor.
@code{*} matches any characters, including spaces and slashes.
A file watch is not a command.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are
command patterns, not paths.

@code{Edit(/src/**)} matches a path inside the project.
@code{*} matches one path segment.  @code{**} matches across
segments.  A leading slash is the project, not the filesystem
root.  @code{/}, @code{c:/}, a drive prefix, @code{..}, and
@code{~} are refused when the rule is read.
@code{WebFetch(domain:example.com)} matches that host and a
subdomain.  It does not match a search.

A deny is checked before the question.  It wins over an allow,
and it still refuses in @code{accept-edits} and
@code{always-approve}, including a read that would not have
asked.  The model receives @samp{Error: Permission rule denied },
followed by the rule.  An allow skips the question when every
command, path, or host matches.  A patch is allowed only when
every hunk path matches.  One denied hunk refuses the whole
patch, and nothing is written.  Plan mode still refuses a shell
command that can write, before these rules.  A read-only command
that plan mode allows can still be denied.

@vindex harmless-dangerous-commands
@code{harmless-dangerous-commands} names programs that are refused
before the question, including in @code{always-approve}.  An allow
rule does not override the list.  The match is the lower-case file
name.  @code{sudo} and @code{doas} are unwrapped once.  A name that
starts with @code{mkfs.} is refused.  @code{rm} of @code{/}, a drive
root, @code{~}, or @code{/*} is refused too.  A longer path is not.
The model receives @samp{Error: dangerous command refused: },
followed by the program name.  In plan mode a command that can
write is still refused as a plan-mode write.

@vindex harmless-auto-review-function
In @code{auto} mode the reviewer runs only when the call would
ask and no allow rule matches.  @code{harmless-auto-review-function},
when set, is that classifier.  It is called with the session,
the tool, and the arguments, and it returns @code{allow} or
@code{escalate}.  Any other return, or an error, escalates.
Nil asks the reviewer model.  The model sees the tool name and
the arguments and is given no tools.  Only a reply that is
exactly @code{allow} or @code{escalate} is kept.  A missing
provider, an error, a tool call, or any other reply escalates.
The reviewer does not run the tool.  The classifier is not saved
on the session.  The mode is saved.  @kbd{M-x
harmless-set-permission-mode} accepts @code{auto}.  Nil uses the
default mode.  An unknown mode is refused: @samp{permission mode
is not a mode}.  A saved mode Harmless does not know is loaded
as @code{ask}.

@vindex harmless-hooks
@code{harmless-hooks} is a list of hooks.  It is not saved on the
session.  A @code{pre} hook can block a tool call before the
question.  The model receives @samp{Error: hook blocked this tool
call}, or that text, a colon, and the reason.  A block does not
run the tool.  A hook cannot override plan mode, the
dangerous-command list, or a deny rule, and a hook cannot skip
the question.  A @code{post} hook runs after the tool and can
replace the result the model sees.  The files the tool wrote stay
as they are.  A hook error blocks before the call and leaves the
result it was given after the call.  A hook is a function, a
command, an HTTP request, an MCP call, a prompt, or a subagent.
Nil, @code{""}, @code{/}, and @code{c:/} are not URLs and are not
fetched.

@node Accounts
@chapter Accounts

@findex harmless-login
@findex harmless-logout
@kindex M-x harmless-login
@kindex M-x harmless-logout
@kbd{M-x harmless-login} signs in.  If several connections exist, it
asks which one.  @kbd{M-x harmless-logout} forgets the Harmless token
for that connection.  It does not delete a token that belongs to Grok
Build, Claude Code, or Codex.

@heading xAI

@vindex harmless-xai-use-grok-auth
Browser login opens @code{auth.x.ai}.  Harmless listens on port 56121.
Tokens are stored under @code{harmless-directory}, mode 0600, and
refresh on their own.  A prefix argument uses the device-code flow.
Harmless also uses that flow when port 56121 is already taken, for
example by Grok Build.

If you have already run @code{grok login} and
@code{harmless-xai-use-grok-auth} is non-nil, Harmless reuses
@file{~/.grok/auth.json} until you sign in or out from Emacs.  It
never writes that file.  A refresh is saved under
@code{harmless-directory} instead.

An API key still works when no OAuth session is active.  Put it in
@file{~/.authinfo} as @code{machine api.x.ai login apikey password
xai-...}, or export @code{XAI_API_KEY}.

@heading OpenAI

@vindex harmless-openai-use-codex-auth
Login opens ChatGPT OAuth and listens on @code{localhost:1455}.  A
prefix argument asks you to paste the redirect URL.  Harmless also
does that when port 1455 is busy.  Tokens go in
@file{auth-openai.json}.  A still-valid @file{~/.codex/auth.json} is
reused and never written.

ChatGPT subscription tokens are sent to the Codex Responses API, not
to @code{api.openai.com}.  @code{OPENAI_API_KEY} remains a usage-based
fallback on Chat Completions.

@heading Anthropic

@vindex harmless-anthropic-use-claude-auth
Login uses Claude Code's OAuth client.  The browser shows a code.
Paste it at the minibuffer prompt.  Tokens go in
@file{auth-anthropic.json}.  A still-valid
@file{~/.claude/.credentials.json} access token is reused.  Harmless
does not write or refresh that file.

@heading Several accounts

Give each account its own connection name.  The default names keep the
original files: @file{auth.json}, @file{auth-openai.json}, and
@file{auth-anthropic.json}.  Any other name gets
@file{auth-@var{slug}.json}.  A session stays on the connection it was
given.  One buffer can use a personal Grok account while another uses
a work Claude account.

A local server has no browser login.

@node Models
@chapter Models

@findex harmless-pick-model
@findex harmless-set-model
@findex harmless-set-reasoning-effort
@vindex harmless-default-model
@vindex harmless-default-reasoning-effort
@cindex reasoning effort
Click the model in the header line, or run @kbd{M-x harmless-pick-model}.
Harmless asks for a logged-in connection, then a model, then a
reasoning effort.  A provider with one model skips the model prompt.
A model that does not take an effort skips the effort prompt.

You can also set the pieces separately.  @kbd{M-x harmless-set-model}
changes the model.  @kbd{M-x harmless-set-reasoning-effort} changes
the effort.  An empty effort clears the session's own value and falls
back to @code{harmless-default-reasoning-effort}.

Models whose names start with @code{grok-4}, @code{gpt-5}, or
@code{gpt-6}, and Claude models, accept an effort.  The usual values
are @code{low}, @code{medium}, @code{high}, and @code{xhigh}.  Claude
also accepts @code{max}.  A model id such as @code{grok-4.6-xhigh}
sets both the model and the effort.

Nil for the default effort omits the parameter.  For grok-4.6, the
provider then uses @code{high}.

The choice is saved with the session.  The next turn uses it.

@node Tools
@chapter Tools

@cindex tools
The model does not change your files by printing a diff.  It calls
tools.  You see each call in the transcript.  Permission rules are in
@ref{Permissions}.

Tool calls in one reply run together.  Harmless asks for permission
one call at a time.  An allowed call starts before the next question,
so several shell commands overlap.  Results are added to the
transcript in the order the model requested them.  Abort cancels the
calls that are still running.

Paths are relative to the project root unless you pass an absolute
path that is still inside that root, or inside a directory added for
this session.  A path that escapes both is refused.  An empty path is
refused.  The filesystem root is not a project: a search there is
refused, and Harmless will not store its own state there.

@findex harmless-add-directory
@kindex + @r{(transcript)}
@kbd{+}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-add-directory}, lets this session read
and edit one directory outside the project.  The same command is
@kbd{+} in the Harmless menu.  The directory is saved with the
session.  A fork and a subagent keep the list.  @code{glob} searches
the project and every added directory.  With no @code{path},
@code{grep} does too.  The shell's remembered directory is still the
project only.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{extra directory
is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{extra
directory must be a string}.  A relative path, including @code{~},
is refused and is not expanded: @samp{extra directory must be
absolute}.  @code{/}, @code{c:/}, and a drive root are refused:
@samp{extra directory is the filesystem root}.  A missing path is
refused: @samp{extra directory is missing}.  A file is refused:
@samp{extra directory is not a directory}.  The project itself, and
a directory inside it, are refused: @samp{extra directory is inside
the project}.  A trailing slash is the same directory.  A detached
chat is refused: @samp{This chat is not attached to a project}.

A path outside the project and outside every added directory is
still refused: @samp{Path escapes project: }, followed by the path.

A detached chat is offered @code{memory_read},
@code{memory_remember}, @code{memory_write_topic}, and
@code{ask_user}.  @xref{Memory}.  @xref{Questions}.
It can list live sessions and send one a finding.
@xref{Messages}.
It can search the web and fetch a page.  @xref{Web search}.
@xref{Fetch a page}.
It can search X for posts, a thread, and accounts.
@xref{X search}.
It can list and read MCP resources.  @xref{MCP}.
It can see the screen, click, type, and open a browser.
@xref{Computer use}.
It can schedule a prompt.  @xref{Schedules}.

@menu
* File tools::  Read, list, write, replace, and patch.
* Search::      Find files and lines.
* Shell::       Run a command.
* Questions::   Ask one question with choices.
* Subagents::   A child session that reports a summary.
* Workflows::   One script, many child sessions, one result.
* Worktrees::   Isolated checkouts of the same repository.
* Messages::    A finding sent to another live session.
* Web search::  Look up a query on the web.
* Fetch a page::  Read one http or https page.
* X search::    Find posts, a thread, and accounts on X.
* MCP::         Call a configured server and read its resources.
* Computer use::  See the screen, click, type, and open a browser.
* Monitors::    Wake when a line in a file or a stream matches.
* Schedules::   Send one prompt at a later time.
@end menu

@node File tools
@section File tools

@code{read_file}, @code{list_dir}, @code{write_file}, @code{replace},
and @code{apply_patch} also accept a path inside a directory added
with @kbd{M-x harmless-add-directory}.

@table @code
@item read_file
Read a project file.  @code{offset} is the first line, counting from
1.  @code{limit} is how many lines to return.  The result is cut at
@code{harmless-read-file-max-bytes} (100000).  A cut result ends with
@samp{[truncated]}.

@item list_dir
List the names in a directory.  A directory name ends with a slash.
The default path is the project root.

@item write_file
Create or replace a file with the given contents.  This is an edit.

@item replace
Replace one unique string in a file.  If the string occurs more than
once, the call fails unless @code{replace_all} is true.  This is an
edit.

@item apply_patch
Apply several hunks in one call.  Each hunk names a @code{path}, an
@code{old_string}, and a @code{new_string}.  The old string must occur
once in that file.  Later hunks on the same file see earlier
replacements.  If any hunk fails, no file is changed.  This is an edit.

In plan mode, @code{write_file}, @code{replace}, and @code{apply_patch}
fail.  The model must write @file{plan.md} with @code{write_plan}.
@xref{Plan mode}.
@end table

@node Search
@section Search

@table @code
@item glob
Find files whose names match a glob.  The pattern may be a basename
or a relative path.  The search covers the project and every added
directory.  A match outside the project is an absolute path.

@item grep
Search file contents for a regular expression.  The optional
arguments are @code{path}, @code{glob}, @code{engine}, @code{context},
@code{type}, and @code{multiline}.
@end table

@cindex ripgrep
@cindex ag
@cindex grep
@code{path} is a directory inside the project or an added directory.
With no path, the search covers the project and every added
directory.  A trailing slash is the same directory.  @code{glob}
limits basenames, for example @code{*.el}.  A hit outside the
project is an absolute path.

@code{engine} is @code{rg}, @code{ag}, or @code{elisp}.  With no
engine, Harmless uses ripgrep if @command{rg} is on @code{exec-path},
then ag if @command{ag} is on @code{exec-path}, then an Emacs Lisp
scan.

Ripgrep and ag skip ignored files and the @file{.git} directory.
They do search hidden files, so a file such as @file{.harmless} is
visible.  The Emacs Lisp scan reads every regular file under the
directory, including ignored files and @file{.git}.

@code{context} is the number of lines to show around each hit.  A hit
is printed as @code{path:line:text}.  A context line is printed as
@code{path-line-text}.

@code{type} is a file type that the chosen engine knows.  Ripgrep
types come from @code{rg --type-list}.  Ag types come from
@code{ag --list-file-types}.  The Emacs Lisp scan has no file types.

@code{multiline} lets the pattern cross lines.  Only ripgrep can do
that.  Ag refuses it.  The Emacs Lisp scan refuses it.

The pattern is required, and it is case-sensitive.  An ag pattern
cannot start with a hyphen.  A long result is cut at the same byte
cap as @code{read_file}.

@node Shell
@section Shell

@vindex harmless-shell-timeout-seconds
@vindex harmless-shell-output-max-bytes
@code{run_shell} runs one command in the project root through your
shell.  The next command starts in the same directory when a
@code{cd} stayed inside the project.  Nil and @code{""} mean the
project root.  A @code{cd} that leaves the project is not kept.
Harmless reports @samp{The shell left the project. The next command starts in },
followed by the directory it kept.  The filesystem root is not kept.
The remembered directory is saved with the session.

@code{timeout} is how many seconds to wait.  When it is omitted,
@code{harmless-shell-timeout-seconds} (120) is used.  A negative
number, zero, or a value that is not a number is refused:
@samp{Error: timeout must be a positive number}.  An empty command is
refused: @samp{Error: command is empty}.  When the command is still
running at the end of that wait, Harmless leaves the process running
and returns a background id with the output captured so far.  It does
not kill the process.  @code{background} true returns that id
immediately.

@code{shell_output} reads one background shell.  @code{id} is the id
@code{run_shell} returned.  Without @code{timeout}, the tool returns
the output already available.  With @code{timeout}, it waits that many
seconds for the command to exit, then returns whatever it has.  This
tool does not ask for permission.  An empty id is refused:
@samp{Error: shell id is empty}.  An unknown id is refused:
@samp{Error: no background shell }, followed by that id.  A bad
timeout uses the same refusal as @code{run_shell}.

@code{shell_kill} stops a background shell.  It asks for permission
like any other shell command.  Killing a shell that is not running is
refused: @samp{Error: shell }, the id, and @samp{ is not running}.

The text returned to the model is cut at
@code{harmless-shell-output-max-bytes} (20000).

Abort stops a shell that is still in the foreground.  A shell that has
moved to the background keeps running.  When it exits, or when it is
killed, Emacs shows a message and a note in the transcript.  That note
is not a message the model sees on the next turn.  The jobs are kept
in memory for this Emacs session.  They are not written to the session
files.

A command Harmless recognizes as read-only does not ask for
permission.  A command it does not recognize still asks.  A redirect
that writes, a command substitution, and a subshell ask.
@xref{Permissions}.

In plan mode, a shell command that can write is refused before any
permission question:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. A shell command that can write is refused}.
Nil, @code{""}, and a blank command are not that refusal.  A
read-only command still runs.  @code{shell_kill} still asks.
@xref{Plan mode}.

@node Questions
@section Questions

@cindex question
@code{ask_user} asks one question.  @code{question} is the prompt.
@code{choices} are the answers.  Emacs asks with completion, and the
answer must be one of those choices.  The tool returns
@samp{The user chose: } followed by that answer.

A missing or blank question is refused.  A missing choice list, an
empty list, a blank choice, a choice that spans lines, and a repeated
choice are refused.  A choice is text.  A value such as @code{/} or
@code{lisp/} is returned as that text.  Cancelling the prompt returns
@samp{The user cancelled the question.}

The tool does not ask for permission first.  A detached chat can use
it.  Plan mode can use it.

@node Subagents
@section Subagents

@cindex subagent
@cindex spawn_agent
@vindex harmless-agent-max-turns
@code{spawn_agent} starts a child session and waits for its last
reply.  The child has its own transcript.  It does not see the
parent's messages.  @code{prompt} is the task.  Nil, @code{""}, and
whitespace are refused: @samp{prompt is empty}.  @code{description}
is an optional one-line title.  A value such as @code{/} or
@code{sub/} is a title, not a path.  @code{worktree} is an optional
checkout name.  The parent project stays where it is.  The child
edits that checkout.  @xref{Worktrees}.

@code{agent} is an optional definition name.  It is not a path.
@code{""} is refused: @samp{agent name is empty}.  @code{sub/},
@code{/}, and @code{c:/} are refused: @samp{agent name is not a
name}.  A name with no definition is refused: @samp{no agent named },
followed by that name.  @code{sub} is a name.  With no file it is
@samp{no agent named sub}.

Harmless reads @file{NAME.md} from these directories, at every level
from your home directory down to the session, skipping the filesystem
root.  A definition closer to the session replaces one of the same
name farther away.  In one directory, later entries in this list
replace earlier ones:

@enumerate
@item
@file{.agents/agents/}

@item
@file{.codex/agents/}

@item
@file{.claude/agents/}

@item
@file{.grok/agents/}

@item
@file{.harmless/agents/}
@end enumerate

A trailing slash is the same directory.  The file may start with
frontmatter:

@example
---
name: reviewer
description: Reviews code
tools: read_file, grep
model: grok-4.6
sandbox: read-only
---
Report findings.  Do not edit files.
@end example

@code{tools} limits the child.  An unknown tool name is refused.
@code{spawn_agent} cannot be one of them.  @code{model} replaces the
parent's model.  @code{inherit} keeps it.  @code{sandbox} is
@code{workspace} or @code{read-only}.  Anything else is refused:
@samp{sandbox must be workspace or read-only}.  The body is extra
instructions for that child.  Each turn lists the available agents
for the parent.  A subagent does not receive that list.

A read-only child may read files and run a shell command that cannot
write.  A tool that can write is refused before the permission
question: @samp{This subagent is read-only. A tool that can write is
refused}.  A blank shell command is not that refusal.  A read-only
child may send a finding to another live session.  @xref{Messages}.
It may also search the web.  @xref{Web search}.
It may fetch a page.  @xref{Fetch a page}.
It may search X.  @xref{X search}.
It may list and read MCP resources.  @xref{MCP}.
A read-only child is not offered computer control.
@xref{Computer use}.
It may watch a file or a read-only command.  A command that can
write is refused.  @xref{Monitors}.
A read-only child is not offered a scheduled prompt.  A workspace
child may schedule one.  @xref{Schedules}.
A workspace child uses the parent's permission mode.

In plan mode, a subagent that can write is refused before the
permission question, including when the mode is @code{always-approve}:
@samp{Plan mode is on. A subagent that can write is refused}.  A
read-only subagent still runs.  A worktree is refused even for a
read-only child:
@samp{Plan mode is on. A worktree is refused}.

The child cannot start another subagent: @samp{a subagent cannot
start another subagent}.  A tool outside its list is refused:
@samp{this subagent cannot use }, followed by the tool name.

@code{harmless-agent-max-turns} (8) is how many provider calls the
child may make.  The next call stops it: @samp{subagent stopped after
}, the number, and @samp{ turns}.  The value must be a positive
integer.

Aborting the parent aborts a child that is still running.  The parent
does not record a summary for that call.  Aborting only the child
returns @samp{subagent aborted}.  An empty child reply is @samp{The
model returned an empty reply.}

The child's sandbox, tool list, and extra prompt are saved with the
session.  The running callback is not.  A detached chat can start a
child with no definition.  A named definition needs a project
directory.

@node Workflows
@section Workflows

@cindex workflow
@cindex run_workflow
@vindex harmless-workflow-max-agents
@code{run_workflow} runs one script.  The script starts child
sessions.  The tool returns one string, the value of
@code{complete}.  The parent transcript does not gain a message for
each child.  Pass @code{name} or @code{script}, not both.  Neither
is refused: @samp{workflow needs a name or a script}.  Both are
refused: @samp{workflow takes a name or a script}.

@code{script} is the text of a script written for this task.  A
value that is not text is refused: @samp{workflow script must be a
string}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused: @samp{workflow
script is empty}.  The script is not Emacs Lisp.
Harmless does not evaluate it.  A form it does not know is refused:
@samp{unknown workflow expression}.  Text the reader cannot read is
refused: @samp{workflow script is invalid}.

@code{name} is a fixed script, not a path.  @code{""} is refused:
@samp{workflow name is empty}.  @code{sub/}, @code{/}, and
@code{c:/} are refused: @samp{workflow name is not a name}.  A name
with no file is refused: @samp{no workflow named }, followed by that
name.  @code{sub} is a name.  With no file it is @samp{no workflow
named sub}.  @code{description} is an optional one-line title.  A
value such as @code{/} or @code{sub/} is a title, not a path.

Harmless reads @file{NAME.hw} from these directories, at every level
from your home directory down to the session, skipping the filesystem
root.  A script closer to the session replaces one of the same name
farther away.  In one directory, later entries in this list replace
earlier ones:

@enumerate
@item
@file{.agents/workflows/}

@item
@file{.codex/workflows/}

@item
@file{.claude/workflows/}

@item
@file{.grok/workflows/}

@item
@file{.harmless/workflows/}
@end enumerate

A trailing slash is the same directory.  The file may start with
frontmatter.  The body is the script:

@example
---
name: review
description: Review the diff
---
(let security (agent "Review security" :sandbox read-only))
(let tests (agent "Review tests" :sandbox read-only))
(complete (join "\n" security tests))
@end example

@code{name} in the frontmatter is the script name.  When it is
empty, the file name is the name.  A file that does not parse is
left out of the list.  Asking for it still reports the error.

The forms are @code{let} and @code{complete}.  @code{complete} is
required and must be last.  A script with none is refused:
@samp{workflow has no complete}.  A form after it is refused:
@samp{workflow has forms after complete}.  @code{let} binds a name
to an expression.  Binding it again is refused.  A name such as
@code{sub/} is refused: @samp{workflow variable is not a name}.

An expression is a string, a bound name, @code{agent},
@code{parallel}, or @code{join}.  @code{(agent PROMPT)} starts one
child.  @code{PROMPT} may be an expression.  Nil, @code{""}, and
whitespace are refused: @samp{prompt is empty}.  @code{:sandbox} is
@code{read-only} or @code{workspace}.  The default is
@code{workspace}.  Anything else is refused: @samp{sandbox must be
workspace or read-only}.  @code{:agent} is an optional definition
name, the same names as @code{spawn_agent}.  @code{:worktree} is an
optional checkout name.  It counts as a write even when the child is
read-only.  @xref{Worktrees}.  @code{:label} and
@code{:description} are optional one-line titles.  @code{(parallel
(agent ...) ...)} starts those children together and keeps their
replies in that order.  An empty list is refused.  @code{(join SEP
EXPR ...)} joins text with @code{SEP}, which must be a string.  A
list of replies is joined the same way.  @code{complete} of a list
joins it with newlines, so the tool still returns one string.

@code{harmless-workflow-max-agents} (8) is how many children one
script may start.  More than that is refused before the first
child: @samp{workflow agent budget is }, followed by the limit.  The
value must be a positive integer.

A read-only child is one whose @code{:sandbox} is @code{read-only},
or whose definition is read-only.  A workflow whose children are all
read-only, and that starts no worktree, does not ask for permission.
A workflow that can write asks, unless the permission mode already
allows edits.  In plan
mode, a workflow that can write is refused before the permission
question, including when the mode is @code{always-approve}:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. A workflow that can write is refused}.
A script that does not parse is not that refusal.  A read-only
workflow still runs.

A subagent cannot run a workflow: @samp{a subagent cannot start a
workflow}.  A definition that lists @code{run_workflow} is refused
the same way.  The child cannot start a workflow or another
subagent.

Aborting the parent aborts a child that is still running.  The
workflow's result is not added.  A detached chat can run an inline
script.  A named script needs a project directory.  Nil, @code{""},
@code{sub}, @code{sub/}, and @code{/} are not directories Harmless
searches.

@node Worktrees
@section Worktrees

@cindex worktree
@cindex enter_worktree
@cindex exit_worktree
@findex harmless-worktree
@findex harmless-worktree-exit
@code{enter_worktree} points this session at an isolated checkout of
the same repository.  @code{name} is required.  Nil, @code{""}, and
whitespace are refused: @samp{worktree name is empty}.  @code{sub/},
@code{/}, and @code{c:/} are refused: @samp{worktree name is not a
name}.  @code{sub} is a name.  A value such as @code{/} passed as
@code{description} on another tool is not a worktree name.

The checkout is created with git, under @code{harmless-directory},
outside the project.  The branch is @code{harmless/} plus the name,
starting at the session's current @code{HEAD}.  Files that are not
committed are not copied.  The same name is the same checkout.  A
second session that enters it sees the files already there.  Two
different names are two checkouts: a write in one does not change
the other, and neither write changes the original project.

Harmless will not store a checkout at the filesystem root, at a
relative path, or inside the project.  Nil, @code{""}, @code{sub},
@code{sub/}, and @code{/} are not a project:
@samp{This chat is not attached to a project}.  A drive root is not
@file{/}.  A directory that is not a git working tree is refused:
@samp{this project is not a git repository}.  When git itself cannot
be run: @samp{git is not available}.  A branch that already exists
is refused: @samp{worktree branch already exists: harmless/},
followed by the name.  A file already at the checkout path is
refused: @samp{worktree path already exists}.

Entering sets the session's project to that checkout and clears a
remembered shell directory.  The session files stay in the directory
they already used.  @kbd{M-x harmless-worktree} asks for the name.
@code{exit_worktree}, or @kbd{M-x harmless-worktree-exit}, points the
session back at the project it left.  The checkout stays on disk.  A
session that was not entered this way is refused:
@samp{this session is not in a worktree}.  A stored return path that
is missing, relative, empty, or @file{/} is refused:
@samp{worktree original is not a directory}, and the session stays
where it is.

The tool is an edit.  Ask mode asks before creating the checkout.
Accept-edits and always-approve do not ask.  In plan mode the
checkout is refused before the question, including when the mode is
@code{always-approve}:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. A worktree is refused}.  Leaving does
not create a checkout, so plan mode allows it.

@code{spawn_agent} takes the same @code{worktree} name.  The child
edits the new checkout.  The parent stays in its project.  A child
born in a checkout has that checkout as its project; it was not
moved there, so leaving says it is not in a worktree.  A read-only
child still cannot write, and it is not offered @code{enter_worktree}.
Creating the checkout is a write, so plan mode refuses it even for
that child.  A workflow agent accepts @code{:worktree}.  That script
can write.  A fork keeps the checkout the session is in.  It does
not create another one, and it can leave.  Aborting a turn does not
delete a checkout.

@node Messages
@section Messages

@cindex send_message
@cindex list_sessions
@code{list_sessions} lists the sessions that are live in this Emacs.
Each line is the id, the status, and the title.  A session that is
only on disk is not listed.  Nil and an empty registry still return
the heading @samp{Live sessions:}.

@code{send_message} sends @code{text} to the live session whose id is
@code{session}.  Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused:
@samp{session id is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused:
@samp{session id is not an id}.  A missing or non-text finding is
refused: @samp{message must be a string}.  @code{""} and whitespace
are refused: @samp{message is empty}.  The sender is refused:
@samp{a session cannot message itself}.  An id that is not live,
including one that was closed, is refused: @samp{no live session}
followed by that id.  @code{sub}, @code{sub/}, @code{/}, and
@code{c:/} are ids, not paths.

The finding is a line in the other session's transcript.  It does not
start a turn, and it does not change that session's title.  A session
that is idle receives it immediately: @samp{Delivered to }, followed
by the id.  A session that is streaming or waiting on permission
keeps it in memory until the transcript can take a line:
@samp{Queued for }, followed by the id.  That line is written before
the next provider call, or when the turn becomes idle or ends in an
error.  Aborting the turn writes a finding that was still queued.
Closing Emacs before that write drops it.  The finding is not a task,
and it is not kept for a session that later replaces this one.

The tool asks in @code{ask} mode.  @code{accept-edits} allows it.
Plan mode does not refuse it.  A read-only subagent may send one.  A
detached chat may list sessions and send a finding.

@node Web search
@section Web search

@cindex web_search
@cindex network
@code{web_search} looks up @code{query} on the web.  The query is
sent to DuckDuckGo over https.  Nothing is written into the project.
Nil is refused: @samp{query must be a string}.  @code{""} and
whitespace are refused: @samp{query is empty}.  A value such as
@code{/} or @code{c:/} is text to search for, not a path.

Each hit is a title, a URL, and a snippet.  No hits returns
@samp{No results}.  At most eight hits are returned.  The same URL
is listed once.

The tool's class is @code{network}.  It asks in @code{ask} mode and
in @code{accept-edits}.  @code{always-approve} does not ask.  Plan
mode does not refuse it.  A read-only subagent may search.  A
detached chat may search.  A failed request is
@samp{web search failed}.

@node Fetch a page
@section Fetch a page

@cindex web_fetch
@code{web_fetch} reads one page at @code{url}.  Only @code{http} and
@code{https} are accepted.  Nothing is written into the project.

Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{url is empty}.
A value that is not text is refused: @samp{url must be a string}.
A path, another scheme, or a URL with a space is refused:
@samp{url must be http or https}.  @code{/}, @code{c:/},
@code{sub}, and @code{file:///} are not pages.  A scheme without
@code{://} is refused too.

HTML is reduced to text.  A script, a style sheet, and a comment
are dropped.  A link keeps its label.  A body that contains an
HTML tag is reduced the same way.  Other text is returned as
text.  Surrounding whitespace is dropped.  An empty page returns
@samp{Empty page}.  A page longer than 100000 characters is cut
and ends with @samp{[truncated]}.

The tool's class is @code{network}.  It asks in @code{ask} mode and
in @code{accept-edits}.  @code{always-approve} does not ask.  Plan
mode does not refuse it.  A read-only subagent may fetch a page.  A
detached chat may fetch a page.  A failed request is
@samp{web request failed}.

@node X search
@section X search

@cindex x_keyword_search
@cindex x_thread_fetch
@cindex x_user_search
@code{x_keyword_search} finds recent posts on X.  The search covers
the last 7 days.  @code{query} is the text, including search
operators.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are text, not paths.
@code{mode} is @code{Top} or @code{Latest}.  Latest is the default.
Top asks X for relevancy.  Latest asks for the newest posts.
@code{from_date} and @code{to_date} are @code{YYYY-MM-DD}.
@code{to_date} is the day after the last day you want.  A date
that is not that shape is refused: @samp{date must be YYYY-MM-DD}.
A start after the end is refused:
@samp{from_date is after to_date}.

Nil is refused: @samp{query must be a string}.  @code{""} and
whitespace are refused: @samp{query is empty}.  An unknown mode is
refused: @samp{mode must be Top or Latest}.  A limit that is not a
whole number is refused: @samp{limit must be a positive integer}.
A limit outside 1 to 10 is refused:
@samp{limit must be from 1 to 10}.  The default limit is 8.

Each post is a handle, a name, a time, a URL, and the text.  No
posts returns @samp{No posts}.

@code{x_thread_fetch} reads @code{post_id} and the posts around it.
Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{post id is
empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{post id must
be a string}.  Anything that is not a decimal id, including
@code{/} and @code{c:/}, is refused: @samp{post id is not an id}.
The result is at most 8 posts, oldest first, and it includes the
requested post.  No posts returns @samp{No posts}.

@code{x_user_search} finds accounts by name, handle, or bio.  The
query and limit refusals match post search.  Each account is a
name, a handle, a URL, and a bio.  No accounts returns
@samp{No users}.

The bearer token is @code{harmless-x-bearer}, or the environment
variable @code{X_BEARER_TOKEN}, or an auth-source entry for host
@code{api.x.com} and user @code{apikey}.  A missing token is
refused before a request: @samp{X is not signed in}.

The tools' class is @code{network}.  They ask in @code{ask} mode
and in @code{accept-edits}.  @code{always-approve} does not ask.
Plan mode does not refuse them.  A read-only subagent may search
X.  A detached chat may search X.  A response that is not JSON is
@samp{X search failed}.  A request that does not succeed is
@samp{web request failed}.  Nothing is written into the project.

@node MCP
@section MCP

@cindex MCP
@cindex mcp_list_resources
@cindex mcp_read_resource
Harmless calls tools on a local MCP server and reads that server's
resources.  Servers are named in @file{mcp.json}.  The project file
is @file{.harmless/mcp.json} in the session directory.  The global
file is @file{mcp.json} under the Harmless data directory.  A name
in both files uses the project command.  A detached chat reads only
the global file.  A relative directory, an empty directory, and the
filesystem root do not select a project file.

The file is a JSON object.  @code{mcpServers} maps a server name to
a @code{command} and an optional @code{args} list.  A name that is
not a single token is refused: @samp{MCP server name is not a
name}.  @code{sub} with surrounding spaces is that token.  A
missing command is refused: @samp{MCP command is empty}.  A command
that is not text is refused: @samp{MCP command must be a string}.
An argument that is not text is refused: @samp{MCP argument must
be a string}.  A file that is not JSON is refused: @samp{mcp.json
is not valid}.  A file with no @code{mcpServers} object is refused:
@samp{mcp.json has no servers}.  A missing file means no servers.

Each server tool is offered as @code{mcp__}, the server name,
@code{__}, and the tool name.  The offered schema is an empty
object.  Harmless loads the server's schema when the tool is about
to be called.  A missing required argument is refused:
@samp{MCP argument }, the name, and @samp{ is required}.  The class
is @code{network}.  A tool from the server is not offered to a
read-only subagent, because that tool can write.

@code{mcp_list_resources} lists resources for @code{server}.
@code{mcp_read_resource} reads @code{uri} from that server.  Nil,
@code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{server name is empty}
or @samp{resource uri is empty}.  A value that is not text is
refused: @samp{server name must be a string} or @samp{resource uri
must be a string}.  A server name that is not a token, including
@code{/} and @code{c:/}, is refused: @samp{server name is not a
name}.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are ordinary resource URIs.  An
unknown server is refused: @samp{no MCP server }, followed by the
name.  No resources returns @samp{No resources}.  A resource with
no text returns @samp{resource has no text}.

The tools ask in @code{ask} mode and in @code{accept-edits}.
@code{always-approve} does not ask.  Plan mode does not refuse
them.  A read-only subagent may list and read resources.  A
detached chat may do that too.  A failed call is @samp{MCP call
failed}.  A command that is not a program is refused before it
starts: @samp{MCP command is not a program}.  Nothing is written
into the project.

@node Computer use
@section Computer use

@cindex computer use
@cindex computer_screenshot
@cindex computer_click
@cindex computer_type
@cindex computer_key
@cindex browser_open
Harmless can see the screen, click, type, and open a browser the
user can watch.

@code{computer_screenshot} returns the width, the height, and a PNG
path under the Harmless data directory, in @file{computer/}.  The
file is not in the project.

@code{computer_click} clicks at @code{x} and @code{y}.  A missing
@code{button} is left.  Nil and a value that is not an integer are
refused: @samp{x must be an integer} or @samp{y must be an
integer}.  A negative integer is refused: @samp{x must be a
non-negative integer}.  A button other than left, right, or middle
is refused: @samp{button must be left, right, or middle}.

@code{computer_type} types @code{text} into the focused window.
Nil and a value that is not text are refused: @samp{text must be a
string}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused: @samp{text is
empty}.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are characters to type.

@code{computer_key} presses one key, such as enter, tab, escape, a
function key from f1 to f12, or one character.  Nil, @code{""}, and
whitespace are refused: @samp{key is empty}.  A value that is not
text is refused: @samp{key must be a string}.  Anything else,
including @code{c:/}, is refused: @samp{key is not a key}.
@code{/} is a key.

@code{browser_open} opens @code{url} in the desktop browser.  Nil,
@code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{url is empty}.  A
value that is not text is refused: @samp{url must be a string}.
@code{/} and @code{c:/} are refused: @samp{url must be http or
https}.

The class is @code{shell}.  The tools ask in @code{ask} mode and in
@code{accept-edits}.  @code{always-approve} does not ask.  In plan
mode a click, typed text, a key, and the browser are refused before
the question:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. Computer control is refused}.
A screenshot still runs.  A read-only subagent is not offered
these tools.  A detached chat is offered them.  Nothing is written
into the project.

@node Monitors
@section Monitors

@cindex monitor
@cindex monitor_stop
@code{monitor} watches one source until a line matches
@code{pattern}.  The source is a @code{path} inside the project or
an added directory, a @code{command}, or the id of a background
@code{shell}.  Pass one of those.  None is refused: @samp{monitor
source is missing}.
More than one is refused: @samp{monitor source must be one}.

Nil and a value that is not text are refused: @samp{pattern must
be a string}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused:
@samp{pattern is empty}.  A regex Emacs cannot compile is refused:
@samp{pattern is not a pattern}.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are
patterns, not paths.

A path is resolved inside the project or an added directory.  A
value that is not text is refused: @samp{path must be a string}.
@code{""} and whitespace are refused: @samp{Path is empty}.
@code{/}, @code{c:/}, and a path that leaves the project and every
added directory are refused: @samp{Path escapes project: }, followed
by the path.  A directory,
including a trailing slash, is refused: @samp{monitor path is a
directory}.  A missing file is refused: @samp{monitor file is
missing}.  Lines already in the file do not match.  Later lines
do.

A command uses the same read-only check as the shell.  A value
that is not text is refused: @samp{command must be a string}.
@code{""} and whitespace are refused: @samp{command is empty}.  A
shell id that is not text is refused: @samp{shell id
must be a string}.  A negative number is refused: @samp{shell id
is not an id}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused: @samp{shell
id is empty}.  An unknown shell id is refused: @samp{no background
shell }, followed by the id.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are ids, not
paths.

A match sends @samp{Monitor }, the id, @samp{ matched:}, and the
line.  The session is idle, so that starts a turn.  A session
that is already in a turn queues the line and keeps going.
@code{monitor_stop} stops the watch.  A command this monitor
started is killed.  A background shell that was only watched
keeps running.  Nil, @code{""}, and whitespace are refused:
@samp{monitor id is empty}.  A value that is not text is refused:
@samp{monitor id must be a string}.  A negative number is refused:
@samp{monitor id is not an id}.  An unknown id is refused:
@samp{no monitor }, followed by the id.  Stopping a monitor that
has already stopped is refused: @samp{monitor }, the id, and
@samp{ is not running}.

A file watch does not ask.  A read-only command does not ask.  A
command that can write asks in @code{ask} mode and in
@code{accept-edits}.  @code{always-approve} does not ask.  In plan
mode that command is refused: @samp{Error: Plan mode is on. A shell
command that can write is refused}.  A file watch and a read-only
command still run.  A read-only subagent may watch a file or a
read-only command.  A detached chat is not offered these tools.
Nothing is written into the project.  The watch is forgotten when
Emacs exits.

@node Schedules
@section Schedules

@cindex schedule
@cindex schedule_cancel
@code{schedule} sends @code{prompt} once, at a later time.  Pass
@code{delay} in seconds, or @code{at} as a UTC time such as
@code{2099-01-01T00:00:00Z}.  Pass one of those.  None is refused:
@samp{schedule time is missing}.  More than one is refused:
@samp{schedule time must be one}.

Nil and a value that is not text are refused: @samp{prompt must
be a string}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused:
@samp{prompt is empty}.  @code{/}, @code{c:/}, @code{log.txt}, and
@code{sub/} are prompt text, not paths.  They are not resolved,
and they do not create a file or a directory.

A delay that is not a positive number is refused: @samp{delay
must be a positive number}.  Zero and a negative number are
refused the same way.  A time that is not text is refused:
@samp{at must be a string}.  @code{""} and whitespace are refused:
@samp{at is empty}.  A value Emacs cannot parse, including
@code{/} and @code{c:/}, is refused: @samp{at is not a time}.  A
time that has already passed is refused: @samp{schedule time has
passed}.

The prompt and the absolute time are saved with the session.
When that session is loaded, a schedule that is still in the
future is armed again.  A schedule that has expired is removed
and is not sent.  This is one prompt, once.  It does not repeat,
and it does not watch a queue or a context wall.

When the time arrives, the prompt is sent.  The session is idle,
so that starts a turn.  A session that is already in a turn
queues the prompt and keeps going.  @code{schedule_cancel}
removes a waiting prompt.  The prompt is not sent.  Nil,
@code{""}, and whitespace are refused: @samp{schedule id is
empty}.  A value that is not text is refused: @samp{schedule id
must be a string}.  A negative number is refused: @samp{schedule
id is not an id}.  An unknown id is refused: @samp{no schedule },
followed by the id.  @code{/} and @code{c:/} are ids, not paths.

The class is @code{edit}.  The tools ask in @code{ask} mode, and
the question shows the prompt, or the id when cancelling.
@code{accept-edits} and @code{always-approve} do not ask.  Plan
mode does not refuse scheduling.  The turn that runs later still
obeys plan mode.  A read-only subagent is not offered these
tools.  A workspace subagent may schedule.  A detached chat is
offered them.  Nothing is written into the project.  Forking a
session does not copy its schedules.

@node Instructions
@chapter Instructions

@cindex project instructions
@cindex AGENTS.md
@cindex HARMLESS.md
@cindex CLAUDE.md
@vindex harmless-claude-md
Harmless reads these names in each directory, from your home directory
down to the session directory:

@enumerate
@item
@file{CLAUDE.md}, when @code{harmless-claude-md} says to read it

@item
@file{AGENTS.md}

@item
@file{HARMLESS.md}

@item
@file{.harmless/HARMLESS.md}
@end enumerate

@code{harmless-claude-md} has three values.  @code{nil} never reads
@file{CLAUDE.md}.  @code{t} always reads it.  @code{anthropic}, the
default, reads it only when the session model id starts with
@code{claude-}.  A model such as @code{grok-4.6} does not match.
@file{CLAUDE.md} is read before the other three names, so those later
files win when they disagree.

A file applies to its directory and everything under it.  Outer
directories come first.  At one directory, the files are read in the
order above.  When two files disagree, the later one wins.  Empty
files are skipped.  The walk stops at your home directory and at the
filesystem root.

An instruction file can include another file by writing
@code{@@path}.  The path is relative to the file that contains the
import, not the session directory.  A backslash before a space is part
of the path.  The file's text replaces the import.  An import inside
inline code or a fenced code block is left as written.  A quoted path
is left as written.  The file must stay inside the session directory.
Harmless refuses an empty path, a missing file, a directory, a cycle,
and a path that leaves the session.  The filesystem root is not a
directory an import may enter.

The text is sent with every turn.  It is not copied into the
transcript.  A detached chat does not walk @code{default-directory}
looking for these files.

@node Skills
@chapter Skills

@cindex skills
@cindex SKILL.md
A skill is a directory that contains @file{SKILL.md}.  The file starts
with @code{name} and @code{description} in YAML frontmatter.  If the
name is missing, Harmless uses the directory name.  If the description
is missing, it uses the first paragraph of the body.

Each turn receives a catalog: the name, the description, and the path.
The body is not sent until the model reads that file with
@code{read_file}.

Harmless looks for a @file{skills} directory inside these vendor
directories, from your home directory down to the session:

@table @file
@item .agents/skills
@itemx .codex/skills
Codex.

@item .claude/skills
Claude Code.

@item .grok/skills
Grok Build.

@item .harmless/skills
Harmless.
@end table

In one directory, a later entry in that list replaces an earlier skill
of the same name.  A skill closer to the session replaces one of the
same name farther away.  @file{/etc/codex/skills} is included when it
exists, at the lowest priority.  Skills that ship inside the Codex
application, and are not files on disk, are not read.

@node Memory
@chapter Memory

@findex harmless-remember
@findex harmless-dream
@cindex memory
Memory is Markdown under @code{harmless-directory}, in @file{memory}.
Global notes apply to every project.  Workspace notes belong to one
project.  A detached chat has workspace memory unavailable and global
memory available.

Each scope has three directories:

@table @file
@item topics
Curated notes, one file per topic.

@item observations/_inbox
New facts, not yet folded into a topic.

@item archive
Inbox files that @code{harmless-dream} has folded.
@end table

@file{MEMORY.md} in each scope is a generated index.  A turn receives
the index, not the notes.

@table @kbd
@item M-x harmless-remember
Save a title and a note in workspace memory.  A prefix argument saves
it in global memory.

@item M-x harmless-dream
Fold the inbox into topics for this project and for global memory.
The inbox files move to @file{archive}.
@end table

The model uses three tools.

@table @code
@item memory_read
Read one memory file.  @code{scope} is @code{workspace} or
@code{global}.

@item memory_remember
Append a fact to the inbox.  @code{topic} is an optional slug, such as
@code{emacs}.

@item memory_write_topic
Replace one topic file with the full Markdown you supply.
@end table

@node Plan mode
@chapter Plan mode

@findex harmless-plan
@findex enter_plan_mode
@findex write_plan
@findex exit_plan_mode
@cindex plan mode
@kbd{M-x harmless-plan} turns plan mode on for the current session.  A
prefix argument turns it off.  The menu key is @kbd{P}.  The model may
also call @code{enter_plan_mode}, which asks you before it switches.

While plan mode is on, the model is told to explore with read-only
tools, to write the plan only with @code{write_plan}, and to call
@code{exit_plan_mode} when the plan is ready.  @code{write_file},
@code{replace}, and @code{apply_patch} fail.  A shell command that
can write is refused before any permission question:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. A shell command that can write is refused}.
A read-only shell command still runs.  @code{shell_kill} still asks.
A click, typed text, a key, and a browser are refused:
@samp{Error: Plan mode is on. Computer control is refused}.
A screenshot still runs.  @xref{Computer use}.
A file watch and a read-only command still run.  A command that
can write is refused.  @xref{Monitors}.
Scheduling a prompt still runs.  The later turn obeys plan mode.
@xref{Schedules}.
The header line shows @samp{plan}.
@code{ask_user} can still ask a question.  @xref{Questions}.

@code{write_plan} writes @file{plan.md} in the session directory.  The
contents replace the whole file.  An empty plan is refused.

@code{exit_plan_mode} shows the plan and asks:

@example
Plan: a approve and build, s request changes, q quit
@end example

@table @kbd
@item a
Turn plan mode off and tell the model to implement the plan.

@item s
Ask for revision notes, leave plan mode on, and send those notes back.

@item q
Turn plan mode off and tell the model to stop without changing project
files.
@end table

Approving does not apply a patch by itself.  The same turn continues,
and later tool calls do the edits.

A read-only shell command still runs.  A command that can write is
refused, including when the permission mode is @code{always-approve}.
@xref{Shell}.

The flag is saved with the session.

@node Usage
@chapter Usage

@findex harmless-usage
@vindex harmless-usage-context-windows
@kindex g @r{(usage)}
@kbd{M-x harmless-usage} shows two blocks.

The session block is the context window of the current model, the
prompt size of the last request, and what remains in the window.  It
then shows the running prompt and completion totals for this chat.
Window sizes that Harmless does not learn from the provider live in
@code{harmless-usage-context-windows}.  A model with no known window
is marked unknown.  Before the next prompt, Harmless compacts the
transcript when that prompt size has reached
@code{harmless-compact-threshold} percent of the window.
@xref{Transcript}.

The account block is the last rate-limit remainder each provider sent.
A connection with no such headers says so.  For the connection named
@samp{xAI}, the buffer also asks xAI for the current allowance when
you are not in batch Emacs.  This report is not an account credit
balance.

In the usage buffer, @kbd{g} redraws and @kbd{q} quits.

@node Files
@chapter Files

@vindex harmless-directory
@cindex session files
Harmless keeps its own files under @code{harmless-directory}.  The
default is @file{harmless/} in @code{user-emacs-directory}.  Nil and
the empty string mean that same default.  A relative path is refused.
The filesystem root is refused.  A missing directory is created when
it is needed.  It is not replaced with @file{/}.

@table @file
@item config.el
Optional configuration, loaded once.

@item auth.json
@itemx auth-openai.json
@itemx auth-anthropic.json
@itemx auth-@var{slug}.json
OAuth tokens for each connection.  Mode 0600.

@item sessions/@var{project}/@var{id}/summary.json
The session's model, status, totals, plan flag, and schedules
that are still waiting.

@item sessions/@var{project}/@var{id}/messages.jsonl
The transcript, one JSON object per line.  A finding from another
live session is one of those lines.  @xref{Messages}.

@item sessions/@var{project}/@var{id}/plan.md
The plan, when plan mode has written one.

@item sessions/_detached/@var{id}/
A chat with no project.

@item worktrees/@var{repository}/@var{name}/
An isolated checkout of that repository.  @xref{Worktrees}.

@item memory/global/
@itemx memory/workspaces/@var{project}/
Notes.  @xref{Memory}.

@item computer/
Screenshots from @code{computer_screenshot}.  @xref{Computer use}.
@end table

@var{project} in a session path is the project directory encoded as one
file name, not the project name you see in the buffer.

The log is not a file.  It is the buffer @code{*harmless-log*}.
@xref{Trouble}.

@node Keys
@chapter Keys

@findex harmless-menu
@kindex h @r{(Harmless buffers)}
@kbd{M-x harmless-menu} opens a transient with the common commands.
You can also open it with @kbd{m} in the transcript or @kbd{C-c C-k}
in the prompt.

@table @kbd
@item n
New session.

@item C
Detached chat.

@item c
Current project.

@item s
Switch session.

@item d
Dashboard.

@item a
Abort.

@item k
Compact the transcript.

@item w
Rewind to the turn at point.

@item E
Edit the prompt at point and rerun from there.

@item f
Fork through the turn at point.

@item +
Add a directory outside the project.  In the transcript buffer,
@kbd{+} runs @code{harmless-add-directory}.

@item P
Plan mode.

@item u
Usage.

@item m
Model.  In the transcript buffer, @kbd{m} opens this menu and
@kbd{M} picks a model directly.

@item e
Reasoning effort.

@item p
Permission mode.

@item l
Log in.

@item o
Log out.

@item r
Remember a note.

@item D
Dream: fold memory.

@item h
This manual.
@end table

@kbd{h} also opens this manual from the transcript, the dashboard, and
the usage buffer.

Dashboard keys:

@table @kbd
@item RET
Open the session on this line.

@item n
New session.

@item g
Refresh the list.

@item q
Quit.

@item h
This manual.
@end table

@node Trouble
@chapter Trouble

@findex harmless-abort
@findex harmless-reload-all-harmless
@cindex log
@kbd{M-x harmless-abort}, or @kbd{a} in the transcript, or @kbd{C-c C-a}
in the prompt, cancels the HTTP request or a foreground shell command.
A background shell keeps running.  A subagent that is still running is
aborted too, and its summary is not added to the transcript.  A
workflow that is still running is aborted the same way, and its one
result is not added.  A checkout that session entered stays on disk.
The session returns to idle and the transcript
records @samp{aborted}.

An empty reply from the model is an error: @samp{The model returned an
empty reply.}

Diagnostic lines go to the buffer @code{*harmless-log*}.  The buffer
keeps at most @code{harmless-log-max-lines} lines (2000).  Do not put
an API key in a note you expect to see there.  The log is not written
to disk.

@kbd{M-x harmless-reload-all-harmless} loads every @file{.el} file
beside the @file{harmless.el} Emacs actually loaded.  It skips
@file{harmless-autoloads.el} and prefers a newer source file over a
byte-compiled copy.  Restart Emacs when a live session was created
before a session field was added.  Reloading does not rebuild this
manual.  @xref{Manual}.

@node Manual
@chapter Manual

@findex harmless-info
@cindex Info manual
This book is an Info manual.  Open it with @kbd{M-x harmless-info},
with @kbd{h} in a Harmless buffer, or with @kbd{h} in the Harmless
menu.  After Harmless has loaded, @kbd{C-h i} lists it in the Emacs
directory.

The source is @file{doc/harmless.texi} next to @file{lisp}.  The file
Emacs reads is @file{doc/harmless.info}.  Rebuild it from the
repository root:

@example
make manual
@end example

That needs @command{makeinfo}, from the Texinfo package.  Commit both
the Texinfo source and the generated Info file.  A change to a
command, a key, a tool, or a permission belongs in the manual in the
same change.

